In a Tibetan Buddhist monastery, a group of monks bends over a flat wooden
platform for days at a time, tapping fine streams of colored sand from metal
funnels called chak-pur. Grain by grain, a vast circular palace takes shape:
gates at the four directions, concentric rings of color, a deity or principle
at the exact center. Then, when it is finished, they sweep it into a single
gray pile and pour it into a river.

## The work of weeks

A large sand mandala can take a team of monks two weeks or more to complete.
The design is not improvised. Every proportion is fixed by tradition, memorized
and chanted as it is laid down, so that the finished image is less a painting
than a diagram recited in colored sand.

## The teaching of the sweep

The dissolution is the point. The sand mandala exists to be destroyed, a
deliberate enactment of impermanence: nothing made, however beautiful, is meant
to last. The sweeping is performed with the same care as the making.

## Reading the palace

Read as architecture, the mandala is a palace seen from above. Read as
practice, it is a map of the path from the outer world to the still center, and
back again.

